# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#
# ShellCheck configuration. `shellcheck .devcontainer/*.sh scripts/*.sh` is the
# whole usage; the tool is in the dev container (.devcontainer/Dockerfile).
#
# Five scripts, all `#!/usr/bin/env bash`, and all of them do things that are
# painful to debug when they go subtly wrong: post-create.sh re-chowns the
# persisted volumes and re-aligns the kvm/docker GIDs, device-test.sh drives a
# real phone, adb-connect-host.sh resolves the Windows host across two WSL
# networking modes. None of it is covered by a test, so static analysis is the
# only check they have. `src/androidTest/` was the same gap one directory over
# until 2026-08-14, when ktlint and detekt were pointed at it; these scripts are
# now the only thing in the repository with no other net under them.
#
# WHY ONLY FOUR OPTIONAL CHECKS. ShellCheck ships nine off-by-default checks. The
# four enabled below are the ones the existing scripts **already pass**, so they
# cost nothing today and act as a ratchet tomorrow. Measured, not guessed — the
# other five would report, on the current scripts:
#
#   require-variable-braces     45  ${x} everywhere, a pure restyle
#   check-extra-masked-returns  13  mostly $(...) inside strings
#   require-double-brackets     11  [ ] -> [[ ]]
#   check-set-e-suppressed       4
#   check-unassigned-uppercase   1
#
# Each is defensible and none is worth a 75-hunk diff across working scripts
# nobody asked to touch. Enable one and fix its findings as a deliberate change
# if it ever earns its place; do not enable all nine and then add `disable=` lines
# to make the result quiet, which is how a linter stops meaning anything.
#
# NOTHING IS DISABLED HERE, deliberately, and nothing needs to be: the scripts
# are clean at default severity. The two findings this file was written alongside
# — an SC2015 `A && B || C` in post-create.sh, an SC2012 `ls` parse in
# device-test.sh — were **fixed** rather than configured away, which is the only
# reason `shellcheck` is worth running in CI at all. Keep it that way: a `disable=`
# line added to make a build green is a finding that stopped being reported, not
# one that stopped being true.

enable=add-default-case
enable=avoid-nullary-conditions
enable=deprecate-which
enable=quote-safe-variables
